import{_ as e,o as n,c as s,a3 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const d=JSON.parse('{"title":"Automatic Balancing Explanation: Synchronized Voiceover Script","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"自动配平讲解-对轴配音稿","archivedAt":"2026-10-31","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=w6t5TkRbD3DI8kV9oOGObx","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/automatic-balancing-voiceover-script"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/automatic-balancing-voiceover-script"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/automatic-balancing-voiceover-script"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/automatic-balancing-voiceover-script"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"automatic-balancing-voiceover-script.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-w6t5TkRbD3DI8kV9oOGObx.md"}'),t={name:"automatic-balancing-voiceover-script.md"};function i(p,a,o,l,c,u){return n(),s("div",null,[...a[0]||(a[0]=[r('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="automatic-balancing-explanation-synchronized-voiceover-script" tabindex="-1">Automatic Balancing Explanation: Synchronized Voiceover Script <a class="header-anchor" href="#automatic-balancing-explanation-synchronized-voiceover-script" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Automatic Balancing Explanation: Synchronized Voiceover Script&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>w6t5TkRbD3DI8kV9oOGObx</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=w6t5TkRbD3DI8kV9oOGObx" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-T0udFqQDsi3HUVmGZAEktB"></span></p><h2 id="synchronized-voiceover-script" tabindex="-1">Synchronized Voiceover Script <a class="header-anchor" href="#synchronized-voiceover-script" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Synchronized Voiceover Script&quot;">​</a></h2><p><span id="source-block-TdmTliJ9pTQA0kZdGXquc2"></span></p><h3 id="_0-why-automatic-balancing" tabindex="-1">0. Why Automatic Balancing? <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0-why-automatic-balancing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;0. Why Automatic Balancing?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>At the start of version 1.4, gas-based industry brought us two mighty figures:</p><p><strong>&lt;（°0°）&gt;</strong><span class="mark-red"><strong>Lord Pyrrolite</strong></span>** and <strong><span class="mark-green"><strong>Lord Heavy Xiranite</strong></span></strong>\\（°0°）/**</p><p>My goodness, <span class="mark-red">Lord Pyrrolite</span> sells for 70 Stock Bills! Its extraordinarily elaborate production process is breathtaking!</p><p>After content creators worked their respective mathematical magic,</p><p>we now know that maximum output means making lots of <span class="mark-red">Lord Pyrrolite</span> and <span class="mark-green">Lord Heavy Xiranite</span>, along with two former favorites, <span class="mark-orange">Lord SC Wuling Battery</span> and <span class="mark-blue">Yazhen Syringe—er, the little one</span>.</p><p>Ideally, no <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> or <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> should be left over, and the same goes for <span class="mark-orange">Originium Ore</span> and <span class="mark-blue">Ferrium</span>.</p><p>To achieve this, creators first proposed a balanced combination of 14.4 <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Parts</span> and 24 <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>, then pushed further to 14.2 <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Parts</span> and 26 <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>. The latter is very, very close to the theoretical solution!</p><p>But I am just an ordinary player. Rather than squeezing out maximum output, I want to ask:</p><p>What if I need medicine for my own use?</p><p>What if I have not connected all the ore deposits?</p><p>What if I do not want to connect the Purification Node?</p><p>What if I have not unlocked over-capacity transfer in Valley IV?</p><p>Hey, ordinary player, that is quite a list of demands. Why not use a lower-output line that leaves more materials unused?</p><p>So what? I want to use all my materials, get high output, and meet those requirements. Is that so much to ask?</p><p><strong>And so, through our efforts, the automatically balanced production line was born.</strong></p><hr><p><span id="source-block-A6YtcrIbSsShc6LyLEsu02"></span></p><h3 id="_1-local-automatic-balancing" tabindex="-1">1. Local Automatic Balancing <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-local-automatic-balancing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Local Automatic Balancing&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Understanding automatic balancing from four inputs into four outputs all at once is a bit much. Let us start by making only Pyrrolite and Heavy Xiranite.</p><p>Although the recipes for <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Parts</span> and <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span> are complex, do not let them intimidate you. Ultimately, they use the same two inputs: <span class="mark-red"><strong>Cuprium</strong></span> and <span class="mark-green"><strong>Xiranite</strong></span>.</p><p>Let us set aside all the intermediate twists and turns.</p><p>Now imagine an account with these resources:</p><ul><li><p><span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> production: <u><strong>325</strong></u>/min;</p></li><li><p><span class="mark-green">Xiranite and Xiragen</span> production maxed out;</p></li></ul><p>We make only <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> and <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>, giving this output:</p><p>Now connect the remaining deposits, increasing <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> production to 510/min.</p><p>How do we consume this extra <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>? Suppose we increase <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span> output…</p><p>Wait, wait—why did <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> consumption decrease? What about increasing <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> output instead?</p><p>That is it! All the extra <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> is now consumed.</p><p><strong>To balance the additional <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>, <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> output must increase and <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span> output must decrease accordingly. (Consider giving this the entire screen.)</strong></p><p>For automatic balancing, however, the line itself must increase <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> output and reduce <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span> output as <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> supply increases.</p><p>How can we do that? Here is the answer first:</p><ul><li><p><strong><span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>: fully supply the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span> first; send the remainder to the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span>.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong><span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>: fully supply the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> first; send the remainder to the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span>.</strong></p></li></ul><hr><p><span id="source-block-jZOd3dBgugDu3i35khYrim"></span></p><h3 id="_2-supply-order-must-not-be-reversed" tabindex="-1">2. Supply Order Must Not Be Reversed <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-supply-order-must-not-be-reversed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Supply Order Must Not Be Reversed&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Why must “<span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> fully supply the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span> first, and <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> fully supply the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> first”? Let us examine each.</p><p><span id="source-block-cYHhsyKNC4L0Lf3Ndagu19"></span></p><h4 id="if-the-pyrrolite-line-lacks-xiranite" tabindex="-1">If the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite Line</span> Lacks <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-the-pyrrolite-line-lacks-xiranite" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If the &lt;span class=&quot;mark-red&quot;&gt;Pyrrolite Line&lt;/span&gt; Lacks &lt;span class=&quot;mark-green&quot;&gt;Xiranite&lt;/span&gt;&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Return to the previous example, just after connecting the deposits:</p><p>We try increasing <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> output, but insufficient <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> supply stops us halfway!</p><p>We can now see that not all the additional <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> is consumed.</p><p><strong>To use the extra <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>, we must first fully supply the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span> with <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>, ensuring it has enough.</strong></p><p><span id="source-block-fX5Pw22uV8xI3PunEWO21N"></span></p><h4 id="if-the-heavy-xiranite-line-lacks-cuprium" tabindex="-1">If the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite Line</span> Lacks <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-the-heavy-xiranite-line-lacks-cuprium" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If the &lt;span class=&quot;mark-green&quot;&gt;Heavy Xiranite Line&lt;/span&gt; Lacks &lt;span class=&quot;mark-red&quot;&gt;Cuprium&lt;/span&gt;&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Similarly, increase <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> supply and then try increasing <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span> output.</p><p>The result is similar: insufficient <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> supply to the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> prevents us from using all the extra <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>.</p><p>Just as when the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span> lacks <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>, using all additional <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> requires fully supplying the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> with <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> first.</p><p>This explains why “<span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> fully supplies the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span> first, and <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> fully supplies the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> first.” But there is another part to our answer: “Send remaining <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> to the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span>, and remaining <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> to the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span>.”</p><p>If we produced only <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>, its line could not consume all that <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>.</p><p>The remaining <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> must be consumed by the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span>.</p><p>To use every input completely, we need a line dedicated to consuming its remainder.<br> For convenience, we call a line that consumes “a particular input left over by other lines” a</p><p><strong>terminal line</strong>.</p><p>The <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span> is therefore the terminal line for <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>: the last line supplied among those consuming <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>.</p><p>Likewise, the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> consumes <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> left over when producing only <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span>, so it is the terminal line for <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>.</p><hr><p><span id="source-block-C4dJPcSew5kq8oedDEKmS7"></span></p><h3 id="_3-more-inputs" tabindex="-1">3. More Inputs!? <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-more-inputs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. More Inputs!?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>So far we have handled two inputs, <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> and <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>, and two outputs, <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> and <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>.</p><p>But version 1.5 also has two other inputs, <span class="mark-orange">Originium Ore</span> and <span class="mark-blue">Ferrium</span>, and two other outputs, <span class="mark-orange">SC Wuling Batteries</span> and <span class="mark-blue">basic medicine</span>.</p><p>Now add them in:</p><p>Does that suddenly look overwhelming? Take it slowly. Our goal is to consume every input, so each input needs a line to consume what the other lines leave over. <strong>We can pair every input with a terminal!</strong></p><p>Attentive viewers may have noticed the colors used for inputs and outputs earlier. Matching colors identify each input and its corresponding terminal:</p><p><span class="mark-red">Cuprium–Pyrrolite</span> <span class="mark-green">Xiranite–Heavy Xiranite</span> <span class="mark-orange">Originium Ore–SC Wuling Battery</span> <span class="mark-blue">Ferrium–basic medicine</span></p><p>We already know that the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span>, primarily consuming <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>, is its terminal, while the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span>, primarily consuming <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>, is its terminal.</p><p>Then the <span class="mark-orange">SC Wuling Battery line</span>, primarily consuming <span class="mark-orange">Originium Ore</span>, is that input&#39;s terminal. The <span class="mark-blue">basic-medicine line</span>, primarily consuming <span class="mark-blue">Ferrium</span>, is the terminal for <span class="mark-blue">Ferrium</span>!</p><p>As with <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> and <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>, allowing the <span class="mark-orange">SC Wuling Battery line</span> to consume all remaining <span class="mark-orange">Originium Ore</span>, and the basic-medicine line to consume all remaining Ferrium, requires fully supplying the battery line&#39;s other inputs first: its <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> and Ferrium must have priority.</p><p>The <span class="mark-blue">basic-medicine line</span> consumes only <span class="mark-blue">Ferrium</span>, so it has no other input that needs priority supply.</p><p>We can summarize this in a table:</p><p><span id="source-block-2eRoxdJJTl9mAKahWoUH50"></span></p><h3 id="_4-more-varied-terminals" tabindex="-1">4. More Varied Terminals <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-more-varied-terminals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. More Varied Terminals&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Must a terminal be a single product? Of course not!</p><p>For example, in 1.5 Automatic Maximum Gross Production, before <span class="mark-red">Hetonite</span> storage fills, <span class="mark-red">Hetonite</span> and <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span> output remains at 1:2. The <span class="mark-red">Cuprium terminal</span> is then not simply <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span>, but <strong><span class="mark-red">“Hetonite and Pyrrolite at 1:2.”</span></strong></p><p>A terminal combining multiple outputs can be treated as a product with a custom recipe, just like the earlier single-product terminals.</p><p>Combined-product terminals give us more types of output! Perhaps at the end of this road we will reach the ultimate <strong>automatic balancing for every item</strong>…</p><hr><p><span id="source-block-dLN8xGfLLH66LVRJpqSROR"></span></p><h3 id="_5-recap" tabindex="-1">5. Recap <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-recap" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Recap&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Let us organize what we have covered:</p><p>For automatic balancing as a whole, remember just two points:</p><ol><li><p>Each input has a corresponding terminal. <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> corresponds to <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite</span>, which can consume all remaining <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span>; <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span> corresponds to <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite</span>, which can consume all remaining <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>. A terminal can also combine products, such as the preceding section&#39;s <span class="mark-red">“Hetonite and Pyrrolite at 1:2”</span> as a <span class="mark-red">Cuprium terminal</span>.</p></li><li><p>Fully supply every other line using an input before sending the remainder to its terminal. For example, <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> first fully supplies the <span class="mark-green">Heavy Xiranite line</span> so it can consume all remaining <span class="mark-green">Xiranite</span>; only then does leftover <span class="mark-red">Cuprium</span> go to the <span class="mark-red">Pyrrolite line</span>, its terminal. The rule is <strong>“supply non-terminals first.”</strong> We will refer to it repeatedly in future automatic-balancing discussions.</p></li></ol><p><strong>“Supply non-terminals first”</strong> is the iron rule of automatic balancing. Next, let us implement it with actual in-game structures.</p><hr><p><span id="source-block-809wjGnxF7cyPOuIKsbB9U"></span></p><h3 id="_6-back-to-actual-production-lines" tabindex="-1">6. Back to Actual Production Lines <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-back-to-actual-production-lines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Back to Actual Production Lines&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Start with the simplest input, Ferrium. It is used by the SC Wuling Battery line and the basic-medicine line. The medicine line ensures all remaining Ferrium is consumed, making it the terminal. We must therefore fully supply the non-terminal SC Wuling Battery line first.</p><p>Only after the battery line is fully supplied with Ferrium will the surplus overflow into a box, enter the depot, and then become basic medicine.</p><p>Of course, many methods can implement “supply xx first, then xx.” Our later production-line release videos will explain how to supply non-terminals first. For example, we will later post a separate explanation of the “empty storage–oversupply” method used in the familiar 1.5 maximum-gross-output video (show its thumbnail). The revised lines to be released later use “full storage–oscillation,” “full storage–overflow,” “empty storage–direct connection,” and “quantified Depot Unloaders.” Explanation videos will accompany them. We hope viewers learn more AIC design ideas from our work and apply them to their own setups!</p><p><span id="source-block-60kuKbNnWvd4OISdYiWDGc"></span></p><h3 id="_7-why-can-you-add-your-own-lines" tabindex="-1">7. Why Can You Add Your Own Lines? <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-why-can-you-add-your-own-lines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Why Can You Add Your Own Lines?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Let us finish the video with something simple and relax.</p><p>Remember the “ordinary-player requirements” at the beginning?</p><p>Most of what we have discussed concerns how the line balances itself. But if you manually add another production line, how does the automatically balanced system handle its material consumption?</p><p>Simply treat the materials consumed by your added line as never entering the automatically balanced system, like unconnected deposits. Once that consumption is excluded, the automatically balanced line turns all the remaining materials into the desired outputs.</p><p><strong>And so, the dream of having it all becomes reality.</strong></p><p>But automatic balancing still has limits. Let us leave a question to think about:<br> How many extra lines—or what input proportions—will prevent the automatically balanced system from fully balancing the remaining materials?</p><hr><p>That concludes our explanation of automatic balancing and adaptive production lines.</p><p>We hope it gives you some ideas.</p></div>',1)])])}const h=e(t,[["render",i]]);export{d as __pageData,h as default};
